00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.800
Okay, so until other people join, let's have some questions.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.300
So I have a question from Tushar.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:26.700
Could you please explain once in short why we calculate SD for historical and noise?

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:27.900
What was the exact need?

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.200
I am a bit confused on this part.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:38.700
So Tushar, we discussed that the problem is that we had to set a threshold.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:39.900
You will remember.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.900
What are we doing in regression testing?

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:48.200
We are testing that our current version is not very useless from the past.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.200
It should be in a particular ballpark.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.000
The value of its metric.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.500
So that's what we test in regression testing.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:04.300
So the criteria to set the window, how much range we will consider it to be okay and how much less.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.800
So we discussed two or three ways.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.800
One way is to put a hard value there.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.300
That it will be below 10% so I will not accept it.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.800
But what was the problem in our case?

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.800
The problem was that some metrics were performing well in our 95-99 range.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.100
Whereas some metrics are not performing well.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:32.100
So it is logical that in the next iteration, when we run a new iteration,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.100
we will tweak the same parameter which will fix that particular metric which is performing badly.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:44.100
If our faithfulness is performing well, giving a score of 99% or giving a precision of 95,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.100
then there will be no benefit in re-ranking or improving the system prompt.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.100
Our contextual relevancy was lessening.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.399
So where will the benefit be?

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:55.399
That we have to reduce our noise in the context.

00:01:55.399 --> 00:02:02.399
So what we did there, we captured the variation through standard deviation.

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:05.399
So we had two types of runs here.

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:11.400
One was our noise run where our configuration was the same and we ran multiple runs.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.400
We ran it because there is inherently some variation in LLM as a judge.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.400
So we captured it there in the noise runs.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.700
And what were the historical runs?

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.700
Where we were changing our actual configuration.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.700
So we are changing the configuration for contextual relevancy and the other metric which is lessening.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.700
And we are capturing its range.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:39.700
If we change our configuration, then how much difference it makes in our metrics?

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.700
So we calculated the limit and then implemented it in our formula.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.700
So this was the whole flow.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.000
