00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:24.300
So guys you already know that we discussed about guardrails in the last session.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:26.300
And we discussed that guardrails are what?

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:34.300
Guardrails are basically your certain rules or certain boundaries.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:38.300
Which you apply on your LLM based application.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:39.300
Why?

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:42.300
Why is this boundary or need to set these rules?

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:47.300
This is because the core of your LLM based applications,

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:50.300
the central component is your LLM.

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:57.300
And the LLM are your generalists.

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:02.300
Generalists means they give a general type output on every task.

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:05.300
Means they are of Harfan Mala type, all rounder type.

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:07.300
So they can do a lot of work.

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:14.300
But your application is never of a generalist type.

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:17.300
It is of a specific type.

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:21.300
Your application has a specific use case.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:23.300
It has specific users.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:26.300
There is a whole way to work with it.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:33.300
So what you are doing is you are running your application by a journalist which is your LLM.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:36.300
So this journalist can go in any direction.

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:38.300
It has no limit.

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:40.300
We have not set any limit here.

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:46.300
So the criteria to set the limit is set through our guardrails.

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:51.300
In simple words, what are guardrails?

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:56.300
Guardrails are a little bit of our control on this LLM.

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:59.300
We are controlling it a little bit.

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:07.300
And basically we are putting it according to our application.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:09.300
I hope you understood this point.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:13.300
After that we discussed what are input guardrails and output guardrails.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:18.300
And there we discussed another point.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:25.300
That is how your guardrails should be invisible.

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:27.300
To whom?

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.300
To a normal user.

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:32.300
Whereas your guardrails should be opaque.

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:34.300
To whom?

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:36.300
To an attacker.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:45.300
Input and output guardrails are those which are user facing.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:47.300
So they are different guardrails.

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:50.300
You have to deal with human inputs.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:54.300
Whereas your output guardrails are your LLM facing.

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:59.300
So you have to cater to the generated response of LLM.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:07.300
After that we discussed that generally there are 3 modes to implement your guardrails.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:10.300
You can implement your guardrails in 3 ways.

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:15.300
The first method was the rule based approach.

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:22.300
In this rule based approach, you generally write a python code.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:25.300
Which is basically a python function.

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:28.300
If you want to understand it simply, it is a function.

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:34.300
The logic written in the code will be implemented according to that logic.

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:39.300
The second method was the classifier based or machine learning based approach.

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:43.300
And the third method was the LLM based approach.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:52.300
We also studied that if you use a rule based approach, then your cost is minimal.

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:59.300
Because you have to make this python function only once and you can use it again and again.

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.300
What was machine learning based?

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:05.300
Here your cost was of intermediate level.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:09.300
You think a small cost is to maintain the model, to train the model.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:13.300
You need a data set for a certain use case.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:16.300
For which use case you are training this model.

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:20.300
Here your whole data science and machine learning life cycle is being used.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:27.300
But once you have this model, then the cost of running it, its inference cost is very low.

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:31.300
Generally in machine learning, the inference cost is low.

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:33.300
And the size of your model is also small.

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:35.300
You can easily maintain it.

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:39.300
Now let's come to the last type that is your LLM based guardrails.

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:45.300
The biggest feature of LLM based guardrails is that these are highly accurate.

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:48.300
Because you are using LLMs here.

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:50.300
But what is the biggest downside of this?

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:52.300
The biggest downside is its cost.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:55.300
And also latency.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:56.300
Why?

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:59.300
Because you are not hosting LLMs here.

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:00.300
You have not developed.

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:03.300
You are using LLMs through an API call.

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:07.300
So for every API call you are doing, you have to pay a cost.

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:12.300
And because you implement guardrails in both input and output.

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:16.300
So as soon as any query or input comes in your rag application.

